  <h1>Profile</h1>

  <h2>Name</h2>
  <p>Masahiro SADAMURA (<span lang="ja">貞村 真宏 / さだむら まさひろ</span>)</p>

  <h2>Current Position</h2>
  <p>
    Researcher at the Graduate School of Human Sciences, The University of Osaka.
    J.D., The University of Tokyo.
  </p>

  <h2>Brief Biographical Statement</h2>
  <p>
    I am a researcher in legal psychology at the Graduate School of Human
    Sciences, The University of Osaka. My work asks how criminal courts handle
    welfare-related information about defendants — developmental adversity,
    disability, social isolation — and what role such information should play
    where criminal justice meets social welfare.
  </p>
  <p>
    I have published 21 peer-reviewed articles (as of August 2026) in psychology
    and law journals and presented at international meetings including the
    European Society of Criminology. Methodologically, I pair vignette
    experiments and path analysis with natural language processing of Japanese
    sentencing judgments and explainable machine learning (SHAP), building
    evidence at the level of individual judgment and of the system.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bridging law and social psychology, I aim to give Japanese criminal justice
    an evidence-based account of how welfare considerations can — and should —
    enter sentencing. My work has been recognized with awards from the Japan
    Society for Research on Emotion, the Japan Society of Personality
    Psychology, and the Japanese Society for Law and Psychology.
  </p>

  <h2>Research Areas</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Legal psychology (the intersection of law and psychology)</li>
    <li>The role of welfare-oriented support information in sentencing decisions</li>
    <li>Analysis of judicial sentencing texts using natural language processing and explainable AI (SHAP)</li>
    <li>Mixed-methods research (vignette experiments + path analysis + machine learning)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Research Keywords</h2>
  <p>
    Focal concerns theory · Lay judge system · Elderly offenders · Redeemability ·
    Welfare-oriented support · Sentencing decisions · Criminal justice ·
    Explainable AI (SHAP) · Machine learning · Natural language processing ·
    Law and psychology
